EVOLUTION OF THE INTERNET

What it is
The Internet started as a military research network (ARPANET)
and became a global packet-switched communication system
that now connects billions of devices.

Core idea
Store-and-forward packet switching + layered protocols.
Data is split into packets, routed independently, then reassembled.

Timeline / generations
1960s  ARPANET          packet switching, IMPs, first host-to-host
1980s  TCP/IP           internetworking standard (Jan 1, 1983 flag day)
1990s  WWW              HTTP + HTML + URLs + browsers
2000s  Broadband        always-on last-mile (DSL, cable, fiber)
2010s  Mobile Internet  3G/4G, smartphones, apps
2020s  Cloud & IoT      hyperscale compute, 5G, billions of endpoints

Key building blocks
Packet        unit of data with header (src, dst, proto, seq)
IP address    network-layer locator (IPv4 / IPv6)
TCP           reliable, ordered, congestion-controlled byte stream
UDP           unreliable datagrams (DNS, video, games)
HTTP/HTTPS    application protocol for the Web
DNS           name -> address resolution
Routing       BGP between ASes, OSPF/IS-IS inside networks
Wi-Fi / 4G / 5G   wireless access layers under IP

Major changes
- Connectivity: few research hosts -> planetary mesh
- Speed: bits/s on copper -> Gbps+ fiber and 5G
- Medium: wired only -> wireless last hop + mobile
- Access: institutions -> anyone with a phone
- Compute: local machines -> cloud + edge + IoT devices

